## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first PWAs are a common beginner challenge, ensuring service workers and syncing work under real user conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX and service worker caching
- E2E data seeding for deterministic tests
- Network control via intercepts (cy.intercept / route)
- Idempotent sync and duplicate prevention

## Code Example
```javascript
// Cypress example sketch
cy.request('POST', '/api/test/setup', {user:'test', products:[101,102]});
cy.visit('/');
cy.intercept('/api/cart', { statusCode: 503 }).as('cartOffline');
cy.get('#add-101').click();
cy.get('#add-102').click();
cy.intercept('/api/cart', (req) => req.continue()).as('cartOnline');
cy.wait('@cartOffline');
cy.get('#checkout').click();
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for a multi-user scenario with concurrent offline sessions?
- What metrics would you collect to ensure the offline sync is fast enough for a good UX?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for per-user feature flags are common in SaaS apps. This question checks the ability to seed data, perform API-driven state changes, and verify UI behavior across login and page reloads in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Per-user feature flags and API seed/reset
- End-to-end UI verification of dynamic layouts
- Test isolation and deterministic CI runs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example using Playwright-like syntax (pseudocode)
// seedUser(), setFlag(), login() are helper utilities
test('compact dashboard toggling', async ({ page, request }) => {
  const { userId, email, password } = await seedUser(request);
  await setFlag(request, userId, 'CompactDashboard', true);
  await login(page, email, password);
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-compact')).toBeVisible();
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-compact')).toBeVisible();
  await setFlag(request, userId, 'CompactDashboard', false);
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-full')).toBeVisible();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you guard against flaky UI assertions caused by caching?
- How would you extend the test to cover multiple user segments (roles/regions) and parallel execution?

## Why This Is Asked

End-to-end tests for multi-tenant data pipelines must validate deterministic inputs, failure scenarios, and dashboard correctness under realistic conditions. These tests challenge system reliability by combining data isolation, scheduled execution, and eventual consistency requirements.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation via headers or subdomains
- Deterministic test data using fixtures and mocks
- Idempotent reruns and retry mechanisms
- Failure injection and eventual consistency verification
- Tooling strategy between Playwright and Cypress

## Code Example

