## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates understanding of end-to-end testing for real-time media systems at scale, with emphasis on synchronization verification, fault injection, and observability under production-like conditions.

## Key Concepts

- Large-scale WebRTC testing with synthetic clients
- Network emulation including latency, jitter, and packet loss profiles
- Audio-video synchronization verification
- Recording integrity and consistency across media servers
- Burst load testing and stress testing methodologies
- Signaling and media path coordination

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode for WebRTC E2E test harness
class WebRTCE2EHarness {
  constructor(config) {
    this.rooms = [];
    this.networkProfiles = config.networkProfiles;
    this.statsInterval = config.statsIntervalMs || 1000;
  }

  async createRoom(participants, networkProfile) {
    const room = new Room(participants, networkProfile);
    await room.initialize();
    this.rooms.push(room);
    return room;
  }

  async runBurstLoadTest(options) {
    const { roomCount, participantsPerRoom, staggerDelay } = options;
    for (let i = 0; i < roomCount; i++) {
      await this.createRoom(participantsPerRoom, this.getRandomProfile());
      await this.sleep(staggerDelay);
    }
  }

  collectMetrics() {
    return this.rooms.flatMap(room => room.getRTCStats());
  }

  verifyRecordingSync(recording1, recording2) {
    const drift = Math.abs(recording1.timestamp - recording2.timestamp);
    return drift < 50; // 50ms tolerance
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for autosave/drafts cover offline-first UX, idempotent writes, and cross-device conflicts, which are common pain points in real apps.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first data flows and write queuing
- Idempotent REST writes and ordering guarantees
- Test data isolation via seeds and reset hooks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright skeleton (pseudo)
test('autosave offline queue and merge', async ({ page, request }) => {
  await page.goto('/editor');
  await page.fill('#note', 'Initial');
  await page.waitForTimeout(31000); // wait for autosave
  // simulate offline
  await page.route('POST', '/api/drafts', route => route.abort());
  await page.fill('#note', 'Offline edit');
  await page.setOfflineMode(true);
  await page.fill('#note', 'More offline edits');
  await page.setOfflineMode(false);
  // verify upload order and no duplicates
  // reload or inspect draft store
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for WebSocket-based drafting?
- How would you test long offline periods and partial merges?

## Why This Is Asked
Probes ability to design robust E2E tests for streaming pipelines, focusing on exactly-once semantics, replay safety, fault injection, and cross-store validation.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end streaming tests across Kafka, storage, and materialized views
- Exactly-once and idempotent writes
- Replay/failure scenarios and broker partition events
- Tenant data isolation and deterministic mocks
- Observability: SLA validation and latency metrics

## Code Example
